(window.FAIAS_IRQ=window.FAIAS_IRQ||{})["IRQ-CON-010"]={"canvas":[{"n":1,"title":"IRQ ID & Title","html":"<p><strong>IRQ-CON-010: Engineered Friction in AI-Mediated Customer Journeys Reducing Viable Choice</strong></p>\n<p>Register question: \"Where an AI system mediates a customer journey, does its own interaction design — dynamically generated retention dialogue, pre-selected defaults, or the number and complexity of steps — make exiting, switching, cancelling, claiming or complaining materially more effortful than buying or upgrading, and is that asymmetry measured against the acquisition path and across near-identical customers?\"</p>"},{"n":2,"title":"Business Activities Affected","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Conversational servicing and retention:</strong> A chatbot or voice assistant handling cancellation, switching, claim or complaint intake may generate retention dialogue, clarification loops or re-confirmation turns that lengthen the exit path, where the equivalent acquisition path is short and frictionless.</li>\n<li><strong>Agentic journey orchestration:</strong> An agent that composes the steps of a servicing journey at run time decides how many screens, confirmations and mandatory fields a customer passes through, and can compose a longer path for one action than for another without any rule in the journey design saying so.</li>\n<li><strong>Personalisation engines on servicing journeys:</strong> Where a generative or agentic layer varies the journey per customer — which prompts appear, which option is pre-selected, whether a retention offer is interposed — the friction itself becomes a per-customer variable rather than a fixed property of the journey.</li>\n<li><strong>Complaints and claims intake:</strong> PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) names submitting a claim and making a complaint alongside cancelling; an AI intake layer that adds evidence requests or re-statement loops to those paths engages the rule directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Applicability note — predictive components.</strong> Where a predictive propensity or retention score only <em>selects</em> which journey variant a customer is routed into, this IRQ reaches the deployment through the generative or agentic layer that consumes the score; the scoring-side question of differential treatment untethered from cost or risk is IRQ-CON-006's. A wholly static, human-designed journey targeted by a score is reachable by the firm's existing sludge audit — periodic per FG22/5 ¶11.24, and fixed-journey on this standard's own inference rather than on ¶11.24's words — and is outside this IRQ.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":3,"title":"AI Behaviour(s)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Generates the interaction path at run time rather than executing a fixed flow, so step count, dialogue length, mandatory fields and pre-selected defaults are properties of generation and can differ between two runs, two actions, or two customers without a design change.</li>\n<li>Optimises, or is instructed to optimise, for retention, save-rate, conversion or containment — objectives under which a longer exit path is an improvement on the measured objective, not a defect in it.</li>\n<li>Varies its output with the context it is given, so where tenure, product value, retention propensity or channel enter the context, the friction a customer meets can vary with them without any explicit rule.</li>\n<li>Regenerates the journey on each interaction, so a single walk-through is a sample of the system's behaviour rather than a description of it.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Failure Mode","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>The AI system's own interaction design — dynamically generated retention dialogue, pre-selected defaults, or the number and complexity of steps required — makes exercising an available exit, switching, cancellation, claim or complaint option materially more effortful than acquiring or upgrading in the same journey, and this goes undetected because existing testing examines the resulting decision (IRQ-CON-005), the resulting price (IRQ-CON-006) or the disclosure of AI's role (IRQ-CON-008) rather than the structure of the choice environment, and because the firm's own sludge auditing was designed for a fixed journey and does not reach friction that is regenerated per customer.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":5,"title":"Potential Consequences","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Customer:</strong> The customer remains in a product, service or provider longer than intended, abandons a claim or complaint, or accepts a retention offer in place of the exit they sought — a detriment that is real in money and in agency but that presents, case by case, as the customer's own decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Institution:</strong> Breach of PRIN 2A.6.2R(4), a binding rule, on the consumer support outcome, with PRIN 2A.6.4G(2) supplying the regulator's own asymmetry indicator; past-business review and redress exposure across the affected book, because the population harmed is every customer who met the asymmetric path rather than a set of identifiable complainants.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulator/Market:</strong> A thematic or multi-firm finding that AI-mediated journeys reintroduced the sludge a Consumer Duty remedy had addressed at the fixed-journey level — the same shape as the blind spot IRQ-CON-006 already records for pricing, where a rules-based remedy was reconstructed by a model operating below the rule's granularity.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Failure Chain","html":"<pre><code>A generative or agentic layer is placed on a servicing journey and\ngiven a retention, save-rate, conversion or containment objective\n  ↓ (IRQ-MOD-007: metric-versus-intent divergence, if untested,\n     leaves the objective unexamined against business intent)\nThe layer composes the exit, switching, claim or complaint path at\nrun time — retention turns, re-confirmations, pre-selected defaults,\nadditional mandatory fields — while the acquisition path stays short\n  ↓ (IRQ-CON-010 — THIS IRQ: no exit-versus-acquisition effort\n     comparison performed; no paired near-identical-customer probe;\n     retention-dialogue configuration never reviewed against the\n     appropriate-friction / unreasonable-barrier boundary)\nFriction is regenerated per interaction and, where tenure or\npropensity enters the context, per customer — invisible to a\nfixed-journey sludge audit carried out once at launch\n  ↓ (IRQ-CON-005/-006/-008: each passes on its own object —\n     the decision, the price, the disclosure — and none of them\n     measures the structure of the choice)\nCustomers who sought to leave, switch, claim or complain do not, or\ndo so later and at greater cost; the pattern surfaces through\ncomplaints volume, a switching-data comparison, or a supervisory\nreview of the consumer support outcome\n\nControl Breakpoints:\n  • IRQ-CON-010: exit-versus-acquisition effort comparison, paired\n    near-identical-customer probes, and review of the retention\n    dialogue's configuration and of any per-customer personalisation\n    of friction\n  • IRQ-MOD-007: the layer's objective is tested against business\n    intent before the journey is composed against it\n  • IRQ-GOV-003: approval conditions on the journey layer are\n    technically enforced, not only stated in the approval paper\n  • IRQ-HUM-008: a contest route exists and is itself reachable —\n    necessary, and not sufficient, where the path to it is the\n    thing under test\n</code></pre>"},{"n":7,"title":"Why a Domain Expert May Miss It","html":"<ul>\n<li>A conduct SME who has signed off a sludge audit has audited a journey, not a generator. FG22/5's own monitoring expectation places sludge audits among the activities \"more likely to be carried out at regular intervals or on an ad hoc basis\" (¶11.24) — which establishes <strong>periodicity and nothing further</strong>. \"Sludge audits\" appears once in the whole of FG22/5 and is nowhere scoped, so that such an audit examines the journey <em>as designed</em> is this standard's inference from the AI-specific behaviour, not a proposition the FCA states. On that inference, a layer that composes the journey at run time, and can compose it differently per customer, is outside what the audit observed, and nothing in the audit's result signals the gap.</li>\n<li>The asymmetry is invisible one step at a time. Each retention turn, each re-confirmation, each evidence request is individually defensible, and PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) makes it genuinely defensible: appropriate friction included to comply with 2A.6.2R(3) \"would not amount to an unreasonable barrier\". The finding exists only in the comparison — exit path against acquisition path — which no one runs unless they are looking for it.</li>\n<li>The topic reads as already covered. CON-005 tests the decision, CON-006 tests the price, CON-008 tests the disclosure and HUM-008 tests the contest route; a reviewer who has those four can reasonably believe the customer-harm surface is closed. It is not: a system can pass all four and still have composed an exit path three times the length of its acquisition path.</li>\n<li>\"Lock-in\" is a false friend in this register. Wherever the term appears it carries an institutional sense, not a customer one: vendor and embedding-model dependency at IRQ-TPR-006 (\"or lock-in without export paths?\") and its playbook sheet, and a commercial-dependency sense in the DAT canvases (\"lock-in — the commercial-dependency counterpart\") and in TAXONOMY.md (\"lock-in; provider retention/training on firm data; concentration\"). The point is the <strong>absence of the customer sense</strong>, not a single technical sense across the corpus. A reviewer searching the standard for customer lock-in finds IRQs answering a different question.</li>\n<li>Ownership is split. The journey layer is usually owned by product or channel engineering, its objective by a commercial function, and the Consumer Duty outcome by conduct risk — so the one comparison that would surface the failure sits between three owners, each of whom can reasonably believe another has it.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"Assurance Objective","html":"<p><strong>AO-CON-010:</strong> Where an AI system mediates a customer journey, the friction attached to exiting, switching, cancelling, claiming and complaining shall be measured against the friction attached to acquisition and upgrade in the same journey, and against the treatment given to near-identical customers, with every material asymmetry documented and either formally accepted with a stated basis or remediated.</p>"},{"n":9,"title":"Expected Controls (Traced)","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Exit-versus-acquisition symmetry standard in journey design sign-off</strong> — [FCA, FG22/5, July 2022, ¶9.26, p.100] — the firm's own design standard requires it to \"make it at least as easy to switch out of a product, leave their service or make a change, as it is to buy in the first place\", and the AI-mediated journey is assessed against that standard before release and after material change to the model, prompt or objective.</li>\n<li><strong>Bounded retention dialogue, expressed in configuration</strong> — [FCA, FG22/5, July 2022, ¶9.27, p.100] — retention prompts and incentives are permitted, and the bound is that they \"should not unreasonably impact the ease with which a customer could switch or exit from a product or service should they choose to do so\"; the bound is written into the conversational or agentic layer's system prompt, policy or guardrail configuration and tested there, not stated only in a policy document the layer never reads.</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriate-friction register, friction point by friction point</strong> — [FCA Handbook, PRIN 2A.6.2R(3) and PRIN 2A.6.3G(2), in force 31 July 2023] — each friction point in the AI-mediated journey is recorded as either appropriate friction included to \"mitigate the risk of harm and give retail customers sufficient opportunity to understand and assess their options\" or as an unreasonable barrier, with the reason; 2A.6.3G(2) makes this the distinction on which the rule turns, so an unclassified friction point is an unanswered question.</li>\n<li><strong>Lifecycle-action coverage across PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)(a)–(g)</strong> — [FCA Handbook, PRIN 2A.6.2R(4), in force 31 July 2023] — the rule prohibits unreasonable barriers \"during the lifecycle of a product, such as when they want to\" the listed actions, so the (a)–(g) list is <strong>illustrative, not exhaustive</strong>. Each listed action the deployment supports (general enquiries; amend or switch; transfer provider; access a benefit; submit a claim; make a complaint; cancel or terminate) has its path identified and compared, not cancellation alone — and any further lifecycle action the deployment supports is in scope on the same basis, not excluded because the rule does not name it.</li>\n<li><strong>Asymmetry indicators monitored as the regulator states them</strong> — [FCA Handbook, PRIN 2A.6.4G(1)–(2)] — the firm monitors whether its support causes \"prospective retail customers to be prioritised over existing retail customers\" or, on 2A.6.4G(2)'s own head, \"unreasonable delays when retail customers attempt to engage with the firm\" — of which \"disproportionately longer call waiting times to cancel or make changes to an existing product than to purchase a new product\" is the Handbook's stated example. The head is channel-agnostic, so an AI-mediated channel falls within it directly; turn count and step count are how the delay presents there.</li>\n<li><strong>Governance of per-customer personalisation of friction</strong> — Emerging Practice; no published FS standard for recording, bounding or reviewing per-customer variation in journey friction generated by an AI layer was located. Where the layer varies friction by customer attribute, the variation is recorded and justified; where the firm cannot say whether it varies, that is the finding.</li>\n<li><strong>Sludge-audit scope extended to the generating layer</strong> — [FCA, FG22/5, July 2022, ¶11.24, p.116] — ¶11.24 establishes only that sludge audits are carried out at regular intervals or on an ad hoc basis; it does not scope them, and the journey-as-designed characterisation is this standard's inference. On that inference, the firm's existing sludge-audit discipline is extended from the journey as designed to the journey as generated, with the extension recorded; Emerging Practice as to method, since FG22/5 names the activity without specifying it.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"Expected Evidence (Tagged L1–L5)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Assertion that the journey \"was sludge-audited\" with no evidence that the audit reached the AI-generated layer or compared exit against acquisition — <strong>L1</strong>.</li>\n<li>Journey design documentation identifying each friction point in each in-scope path, classified as appropriate friction or unreasonable barrier against PRIN 2A.6.3G, with the retention-dialogue bound expressed in the layer's own configuration — <strong>L2</strong>.</li>\n<li>The layer's objective, reward or instruction set as configured, and the inventory of any per-customer journey personalisation (variant rules, propensity routing, experiment or bandit configuration) — <strong>L2</strong>.</li>\n<li>Non-production walk-through results: step, screen, turn, mandatory-field and elapsed-time counts for each in-scope exit path and for the matched acquisition or upgrade path, with repeat runs at a pinned configuration — <strong>L3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Non-production paired-probe results: matched near-identical profiles down the same exit path, reported pair by pair, with divergence in step count, turn count, default pre-selection and retention-offer invocation — <strong>L3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Independent conduct-risk or internal audit review confirming the comparison was performed, the asymmetries found were classified, and each material asymmetry was accepted with a stated basis or remediated — <strong>L4</strong>.</li>\n<li>Production outcome data: completion and abandonment rates for each PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) action against the acquisition baseline, retention-offer acceptance on exit journeys, and complaint themes on difficulty of cancelling, switching, claiming or complaining — <strong>L5</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":11,"title":"Independent Test","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>In a test or staging instance running the same model version, system prompt, guardrail and journey configuration as the intended production release, walk each in-scope customer-journey path end to end using synthetic or de-identified profiles, and record for each path the number of steps, screens, dialogue turns and mandatory fields, which option is pre-selected at each branch, and elapsed time to completion. Run the comparison in three limbs. <strong>(a) Exit-versus-acquisition comparison:</strong> for each PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)(a)–(g) action the deployment supports — the rule's list being illustrative, not exhaustive, so any further lifecycle action the deployment supports is included on the same basis — compare the measured path against the matched acquisition or upgrade path for the same product, and against FG22/5 ¶9.26's standard that switching out be at least as easy as buying in; because the path is generated rather than fixed, repeat each walk-through at a pinned configuration so that the count reported is the distribution and not a single sample, applying the repeat-run discipline IRQ-MOD-014 establishes for output stability. <strong>(b) Paired near-identical-customer probes:</strong> construct matched synthetic profiles differing only on the attribute suspected of driving personalisation (tenure, product value, retention-propensity band, channel), run each pair down the same exit path, and report divergence pair by pair rather than as an aggregate — an averaged asymmetry conceals the individual case the test exists to find. <strong>(c) Retention-dialogue and personalisation configuration review:</strong> read the layer's system prompt, policy and guardrail configuration, its objective or reward signal, any retention or save instruction, and any variant-selection, propensity-routing or experiment configuration, and assess each against FG22/5 ¶9.27's bound that retention prompts not unreasonably impair the ease of switching or exiting. Classify every friction point found against PRIN 2A.6.3G(1)–(2) as appropriate friction or unreasonable barrier, and record the classification basis. <strong>No production customer, no live account, no real cancellation, claim or complaint, and no retention offer made to a real person: every limb runs against test and staging environments and against design and configuration artefacts only.</strong> — <strong>★1 Confidence — as at FAIAS v1.2.0 (12 September 2026)</strong>. The rating turns on method, not on plausibility. The harm mechanism is not <em>merely</em> theoretically plausible: it is regulation-named — PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) is a binding rule, 2A.6.4G(2) supplies the asymmetry indicator in the regulator's own words, and FG22/5 ¶9.25–9.27 names the practice. What is absent is the method — <strong>no published study audits a live conversational or agentic system's per-customer, dynamically generated friction, and no recognised published testing methodology for it exists: none established</strong> (field 18). The three limbs above are a first-principles construction from the regulator's stated expectations and this register's procedural conventions; Dwork et al. (2012) §1.1 supplies only the conceptual principle that similar individuals should be treated similarly, not a validated procedure, and is cited for that and no more. Materiality is reviewer-set and unvalidated: PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) places the appropriate-friction / unreasonable-barrier boundary in the assessor's judgement and no benchmark exists for how much additional effort is disproportionate, so result quality depends entirely on assessor expertise. Per ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §6, ★1 is a statement about the field and never a reason to skip the test.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":12,"title":"Pass Indicators","html":"<ul>\n<li>An exit-versus-acquisition effort comparison has been performed for every lifecycle action the deployment supports that PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) reaches — the rule's (a)–(g) list being illustrative — with repeat runs at a pinned configuration rather than a single walk-through.</li>\n<li>No material asymmetry is found, or each material asymmetry found is classified against PRIN 2A.6.3G, documented, and either accepted with a stated basis by the accountable owner or remediated.</li>\n<li>Paired near-identical-customer probes show no unexplained divergence in steps, turns, default pre-selection or retention-offer invocation between matched profiles on the same exit path.</li>\n<li>The retention-dialogue bound required by FG22/5 ¶9.27 is expressed in the layer's own configuration and evidenced as operative, not asserted in policy alone.</li>\n<li>Any per-customer personalisation of journey friction is inventoried, justified and reviewed; where none exists, the firm can evidence that rather than assume it.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"Fail Indicators","html":"<ul>\n<li>No exit-versus-acquisition comparison exists; the journey's conduct evidence is a sludge audit of the journey as designed, with no evidence it reached the AI-generated layer.</li>\n<li>A material asymmetry is found and is unclassified, undocumented and unaccepted — the path to cancel, switch, claim or complain is measurably longer or more complex than the path to buy, with no stated basis.</li>\n<li>Paired probes show friction varying with a customer attribute (tenure, propensity band, product value) with no recorded justification, or the firm cannot say whether its journey varies friction per customer.</li>\n<li>The layer carries a retention, save-rate or containment objective with no bound on it in configuration, so FG22/5 ¶9.27's limit exists nowhere the system can act on.</li>\n<li>Production data shows exit-path abandonment materially above the acquisition-path baseline, or complaint themes on difficulty of cancelling or complaining, with no corresponding finding in any prior testing.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"Reporting Language","html":"<p><strong>Pass:</strong> \"The AI-mediated journey's exit, switching, claim and complaint paths were measured against the matched acquisition path and across near-identical customer profiles; no unaccepted material asymmetry was found, and the retention-dialogue bound is operative in configuration. This conclusion rests on ★1 methodology — the harm mechanism is theoretically plausible and is named in binding rule (PRIN 2A.6), and the testing approach is novel and unvalidated. Result quality depends entirely on assessor expertise. This is expert opinion, not evidence. No published study audits a live conversational or agentic system's per-customer, dynamically generated friction, the comparison method is a first-principles construction, and the materiality of an asymmetry is reviewer-set with no benchmark.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Fail:</strong> \"No comparison has been performed between the effort the AI-mediated journey requires to exit, switch, cancel, claim or complain and the effort it requires to buy or upgrade. This is a direct Consumer Duty exposure on a binding rule: PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) prohibits unreasonable barriers to each of those actions, FG22/5 ¶9.26 states that switching out should be at least as easy as buying in, and on the evidence available the firm cannot say whether its own journey meets that standard — still less whether the friction it generates differs from one customer to another.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Partial:</strong> \"The comparison was performed for the cancellation path and found no material asymmetry, but the claim and complaint paths — both named in PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) — were not measured, and no paired near-identical-customer probe was run, so per-customer variation in generated friction remains untested. Remediation should extend the comparison to every lifecycle action the deployment supports and add the paired probe before assurance is given on the journey as a whole.\"</p>"},{"n":15,"title":"Residual Judgement","html":"<pre><code>Assuming controls are well-implemented and monitored:\n  Severity: 2 (individual detriment — a product held longer than\n    intended, a claim or complaint abandoned — generally\n    identifiable and compensable once found)\n  Likelihood: 2 (foreseeable wherever the journey layer carries a\n    retention or containment objective; mitigated by an operative\n    configuration bound and a performed comparison)\n  Detectability: 2 (the comparison, once established as a control,\n    surfaces asymmetry within a reporting cycle)\n  Recoverability: 2 (reversible by prompt, configuration or journey\n    rollback within hours, at some operational cost, plus redress to\n    the cohort the comparison control has already identified)\n  Novelty: 3 (that friction can be generated per customer rather\n    than designed once is an AI-specific insight beyond fixed-\n    journey sludge auditing)\n\nIf controls are absent or degraded:\n  Severity: 3 (breach of a binding rule on the consumer support\n    outcome; past-business review and redress exposure across every\n    customer who met the asymmetric path, not a complainant set)\n  Likelihood: 3 (probable: the commercial incentive is named by the\n    regulator itself at FG22/5 ¶9.25, and a retention-optimised\n    layer has that incentive in its objective function)\n  Detectability: 4 (silent and individually defensible step by\n    step; visible only on a comparison no one runs by default, and\n    where friction is personalised, invisible to a fixed-journey\n    audit entirely)\n  Recoverability: 3 (journey remediation is fast but cohort\n    identification and redress across the affected book is not)\n  Novelty: 4 (a conduct SME who has completed a sludge audit has\n    strong reason to believe the question is answered)\n\nResidual risk interpretation: High priority. The detectability\nprofile is the driver — the failure is defensible at every\nindividual step and exists only in a comparison — compounded by a\npositive belief that existing Consumer Duty work already covers it.\n</code></pre>"},{"n":16,"title":"Risk Weighting Profile (5 Axes, 1–4)","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Axis</th>\n<th>Rating</th>\n<th>Justification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Severity</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Breach of PRIN 2A.6.2R(4), a binding rule, with redress exposure across every customer who met the asymmetric path rather than an identifiable complainant set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Likelihood</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The commercial incentive is named by the regulator at FG22/5 ¶9.25 and is present in the objective function of any retention-, save- or containment-optimised journey layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Detectability</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Individually defensible step by step and visible only on an exit-versus-acquisition comparison; where friction is generated per customer, outside what a fixed-journey sludge audit observes at all</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recoverability</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The journey fix itself is fast — a prompt or configuration change — and what carries the value is §7 anchor 3's own limb: complex exception handling, multi-team coordination and customer communication to identify and redress the affected cohort, at moderate business impact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Novelty</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A completed sludge audit gives positive — and misplaced — assurance that the question is closed; per-customer regeneration of friction is the AI-specific delta and is not what that audit tested</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"n":17,"title":"Regulatory Basis","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>[FCA Handbook, <strong>PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)</strong>, in force 31 July 2023] is the binding rule: a firm must design and deliver support such that it \"ensures that retail customers do not face unreasonable barriers (including unreasonable additional costs) during the lifecycle of a product, such as when they want to:\" — followed by seven actions: general enquiries; amending or switching the product; transferring to a new product provider; accessing a benefit; submitting a claim; making a complaint; and cancelling or otherwise terminating the relationship. <strong>The words \"such as\" make that list illustrative, not exhaustive</strong>: a lifecycle action the deployment supports which the rule does not name is within the prohibition on the same terms, and an assurance procedure that closes the list to (a)–(g) under-scopes the rule. [<strong>PRIN 2A.6.2R(3)</strong>] requires that the firm \"includes appropriate friction in its customer journeys to mitigate the risk of harm and give retail customers sufficient opportunity to understand and assess their options\", and [<strong>PRIN 2A.6.3G(2)</strong>] provides that friction so included \"would not amount to an unreasonable barrier\" — together these make protective friction and unreasonable barrier a single distinction the assessor must draw, not two separate tests. [<strong>PRIN 2A.6.3G(1)</strong>] glosses unreasonable barriers as steps \"unreasonably onerous or time consuming\", \"complex for a retail customer to carry out\" or \"difficult for a retail customer to understand\", and includes \"asking retail customers for unnecessary information or evidence\"; [<strong>PRIN 2A.6.3G(3)</strong>] extends unreasonable additional costs to \"unreasonable exit fees or other charges, delays, distress or inconvenience\". [<strong>PRIN 2A.6.4G(2)</strong>] supplies the regulator's own asymmetry indicator, and its head is channel-agnostic: a firm would be unlikely to meet PRIN 2A.6.2R if its support causes \"unreasonable delays when retail customers attempt to engage with the firm\", \"including disproportionately longer call waiting times to cancel or make changes to an existing product than to purchase a new product\" — the call-waiting phrase is the Handbook's example, not the limb. An AI-mediated channel therefore falls within the head directly, with no telephone-channel analogy required. [<strong>2A.6.4G(1)</strong>] applies equally where support causes \"prospective retail customers to be prioritised over existing retail customers\". [<strong>2A.6.4G(3)</strong>] carries the same indicator into evidence handling, naming unreasonable delays to \"(b) the firm requesting necessary information or evidence from retail customers; or (c) the firm processing information or evidence received from retail customers\" — the limbs an AI intake layer engages where it adds evidence requests or re-statement loops to a claim or complaint path. Currency stated precisely: 2A.6.2R, 2A.6.3G and 2A.6.4G each carry an effective date of <strong>31 July 2023</strong>; PRIN 2A.6.1 carries 26 June 2026 and the section banner records the section as last updated 26 June 2026 (page accessed 12 September 2026).</p>\n<p>[FCA, <strong>FG22/5</strong>, July 2022, \"Final non-Handbook Guidance for firms on the Consumer Duty\", Chapter 9] names the practice and states the standard. <strong>¶9.25 (p.99)</strong>, under the heading \"Sludge practices\": \"there can be commercial incentives for firms to create friction points (often called 'sludge') that deter their customers from taking action in their interests\", and, in the same paragraph, firms \"can fail to give adequate attention and provide appropriate support where customers seek to take action that does not benefit the firm\". <strong>¶9.26 (p.100)</strong> is the Assurance Objective in the regulator's own words: firms should \"make it at least as easy to switch out of a product, leave their service or make a change, as it is to buy in the first place\". <strong>¶9.27 (p.100)</strong> bounds retention specifically: retention prompts and incentives are acceptable but \"should not unreasonably impact the ease with which a customer could switch or exit from a product or service should they choose to do so\". <strong>¶11.24 (p.116)</strong> places the firm-side activity: \"file reviews, sludge audits and focus groups are more likely to be carried out at regular intervals or on an ad hoc basis\" — that sentence establishes the activity's <strong>periodicity</strong> and nothing more; \"sludge audits\" appears once in FG22/5 and is nowhere scoped, so the fixed-journey character of the activity, on which this IRQ's blind-spot finding rests, is this standard's inference and not the FCA's statement. Note the register's own distinction: \"sludge\" is FG22/5 <em>guidance</em> vocabulary; the binding Handbook rule speaks of \"appropriate friction\" and \"unreasonable barriers\", and this canvas preserves that difference wherever it cites either.</p>\n<p>[UNVERIFIED — no FCA or PRA publication addressing AI-generated or dynamically personalised journey friction specifically, as distinct from the general Consumer Duty consumer support outcome cited above, was located. EU Digital Fairness Act and Digital Services Act dark-pattern provisions, and CMA dark-patterns work, were not examined for this canvas and are not relied upon; they remain the obvious next place to look if a fuller regulatory basis is wanted.]</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":18,"title":"Research Basis","html":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>No recognised published testing methodology exists for auditing dynamically generated journey friction in a live conversational or agentic system: none established.</strong> This is the method-class sense of the phrase (PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8), not the finding sense: the field has no test for the question, independently of whether any given firm has a capability. No published study audits or instruments a live conversational or agentic system's per-customer generation of friction, and no search conducted for this canvas located one. That absence is the finding, and it is the basis of the ★1 rating — not any doubt about the harm mechanism, which is named in a binding rule (field 17).</p>\n<p>[Dwork, C., Hardt, M., Pitassi, T., Reingold, O. &amp; Zemel, R., 2012, \"Fairness Through Awareness\", ITCS 2012; verified against arXiv:1104.3913v2, §1.1 \"Key Elements of Our Framework\", sub-heading \"Treating similar individuals similarly\"] supplies the <strong>conceptual principle only</strong> behind this canvas's paired-probe limb: \"any two individuals who are similar with respect to a particular task should be classified similarly.\" It does not supply a procedure. Definition 2.1 is a Lipschitz constraint on a classifier's output distribution; applying it as a comparator over journey step counts and dialogue turns would be an unvalidated transplant, and this canvas does not make it. The paired near-identical-profile probe at field 11 limb (b) is a first-principles construction and is recorded as such.</p>\n<p>[Zac, A., Huang, Y.-C., von Moltke, A., Decker, C. &amp; Ezrachi, A., 2025, \"Dark patterns and consumer vulnerability\", <em>Behavioural Public Policy</em>, First View, DOI 10.1017/bpp.2024.49] is already in this tree at IRQ-CON-003, and is re-used here <strong>as that canvas states it</strong>: \"a randomised experiment (n=2,252 UK adults) using a fictitious investment website with randomised dark-pattern manipulations — found strong evidence all consumer groups are susceptible to manipulative design, but only weak evidence that income, education or age (proxies for vulnerability) increase susceptibility specifically.\" Two limits are carried with it rather than left to be inferred. First, the attribution: as at CON-003's research-basis review of 25 August 2026, that is the canvas's characterisation of the study; the paper has since been read at the primary (3 October 2026), and it reports a final sample of 2,252 complete observations from 2,500 UK participants (§4.1). Second, the scope: the study concerns UI dark patterns, not AI-generated interaction design, so it establishes that manipulative design changes behaviour and does not establish anything about a generated journey. It is cited for the general harm mechanism and for nothing narrower.</p>\n<p>The framing is not novel in this register and the nearest precedent is stated rather than left to be found: IRQ-SEC-012's own ★ line records that \"★1 reflects an evidenced field with no accepted testing methodology, distinct from a weak or poorly-executed test\" (CANVASES_SEC_012.md), which is the same decoupling of the rating from harm-plausibility that this canvas makes.</p>\n<p><strong>Research basis last reviewed:</strong> 12 September 2026 · <strong>review cadence:</strong> Standard (12 months)</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":19,"title":"Related IRQs","html":"<pre><code>Related IRQs:\n  - IRQ-CON-005 (H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access:\n    narrowing of options by an eligibility, pricing or servicing\n    decision — the decision-based counterpart this IRQ is\n    deliberately distinct from; CON-005 §2's digital-only channel\n    limb is the nearest existing coverage)\n  - IRQ-CON-006 (H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access:\n    prices to observed inertia; this IRQ asks whether the system's\n    own journey design engineers that inertia)\n  - IRQ-CON-008 (H.3 Feedback Loops &amp; Transparency: disclosure of\n    AI's material influence — necessary, and not sufficient, where\n    the journey separately frictions exit; TP-CON-008's journey\n    reproduction is the nearest adjacent procedure)\n  - IRQ-HUM-008 (D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability: a genuine\n    human re-decision through a defined contest route; this IRQ\n    tests the effort of reaching that route and the wider journey,\n    not the route's own adequacy)\n\nDelineation — why this IRQ exists alongside CON-005, CON-006 and\nCON-008, on the canvases as written. Each of the three is bound to a\ndifferent object and would need a different artefact and a different\ntest to carry this one. AO-CON-005 is bound to eligibility, pricing\nand servicing decisions and its field 11 is matched-cohort exclusion\ntesting; AO-CON-006 is bound to price and its field 11 is price\ndecomposition and elasticity isolation; AO-CON-008 is bound to\ndisclosure and its field 11 is comprehension testing. A system can\nsatisfy all three and still compose an exit path materially longer\nthan its acquisition path. The nearest existing coverage is stated\nrather than ignored: TP-CON-008 does reproduce the customer journey\nnon-production (&quot;same placement, same typography&quot;) and does ask\nwhether a contest route exists &quot;and how would you access it&quot;; and\nCON-005 field 2 covers digital-only servicing channels that &quot;may\nexclude customers who cannot or do not engage digitally, without an\naccessible alternative route&quot;, with a fail indicator reading &quot;No\nalternative channel exists (or it exists only nominally)&quot;. Neither\nmeasures effort asymmetry between exit and acquisition, default\npre-selection, or retention-dialogue configuration, so the gap\nstands — but it is a gap beside two adjacent tests, not an untouched\nfield, and it is recorded that way. This is the same shape as the\nblind spot CON-006 already documents for pricing: &quot;[t]he FCA's 2021\ngeneral insurance pricing intervention addressed 'loyalty penalty'\npricing through a rules-based remedy (equivalent new/renewal\npricing); a domain expert may believe this issue is 'solved' by that\nremedy without recognising that an AI model can reconstruct an\nequivalent effect through more granular individualised elasticity\npricing that a blunt new/renewal rule does not fully prevent&quot;\n(CANVASES_CON_006.md, field 7 — where the two inner phrases\ncarry double quotation marks, rendered single here for nesting).\n</code></pre>"},{"n":20,"title":"References","html":"<pre><code>[1] Financial Conduct Authority. FCA Handbook, PRIN 2A.6 — &quot;Consumer Duty:\n    retail customer outcome on consumer support.&quot;\n    Provisions cited (2A.6.2R, 2A.6.3G, 2A.6.4G) in force: 31 July 2023\n    Section last updated: 26 June 2026 · Accessed: 12 September 2026\n    URL: https://www.handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/PRIN/2A/6.html\n\n[2] Financial Conduct Authority, 2022. &quot;FG22/5 — Final non-Handbook Guidance\n    for firms on the Consumer Duty.&quot; Chapter 9 ¶9.25 (p.99), ¶9.26 and ¶9.27\n    (p.100); Chapter 11 ¶11.24 (p.116).\n    Published: July 2022 · Accessed: 12 September 2026\n    URL: https://www.fca.org.uk/publication/finalised-guidance/fg22-5.pdf\n\n[3] Dwork, C., Hardt, M., Pitassi, T., Reingold, O. &amp; Zemel, R., 2012.\n    &quot;Fairness Through Awareness.&quot; ITCS 2012, pp.214-226; §1.1 cited from\n    arXiv:1104.3913v2.\n    URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/1104.3913\n\n[4] Zac, A., Huang, Y.-C., von Moltke, A., Decker, C. &amp; Ezrachi, A., 2025.\n    &quot;Dark patterns and consumer vulnerability.&quot; Behavioural Public Policy,\n    First View. DOI 10.1017/bpp.2024.49. Cited as characterised at\n    IRQ-CON-003 (research basis reviewed 25 August 2026); not read at the\n    primary for this standard.\n</code></pre>"},{"n":21,"title":"Four-Test Validation Footer","html":"<pre><code>---\n**Version:** the release version in `README.md`\n**Subdomain:** H.2 (Conduct &amp; Fairness domain, Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access)\n**Status:** Active\n\n**FOUR-TEST VALIDATION:**\n✓ Test 1 (SME Blind Spot): A conduct SME who has signed off a sludge audit has audited the journey as designed — FG22/5 ¶11.24 evidences that sludge audits are periodic or ad hoc, and that such an audit examines the journey as designed is this standard's inference from the AI-specific behaviour, not a proposition in ¶11.24 — and has no reason to test a generative or agentic layer that composes the journey at run time and can compose it differently per customer. Admitted as a contested judgement on the register's own fold-in precedent (AI-TPR-008, AI-RES-007, deliberate gap 8), resolved in favour of a standalone IRQ because the AI-specific delta is dynamic per-customer regeneration, which no existing CON canvas's artefact or test reaches.\n✓ Test 2 (Plausible Harm): Satisfied on the regulation limb of METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md §3a **Test 2**, whose criterion is disjunctive — &quot;published research, regulation, or documented incidents&quot; — while §3a as a whole requires all four tests to be satisfied. PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) (binding rule), 2A.6.3G, 2A.6.4G(2) and FG22/5 ¶9.25-9.27 name the mechanism directly; Zac et al. (2025) supports manipulative design affecting behaviour generally. Method maturity is a separate question and is carried by the ★ rating, not by this test.\n✓ Test 3 (Testable): Answerable by journey walk-through, paired near-identical-profile probes and configuration review in a test or staging environment, against design and configuration artefacts; no production customer, live account or real cancellation, claim or complaint.\n✓ Test 4 (Decision-Relevant): A journey found to make exit, switching, claiming or complaining disproportionately effortful relative to acquisition is not authorisable under PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) whatever its pricing, eligibility and disclosure separately show — the outcome gates authorisation and its conditions.\n\n**Change Log:**\n- v1.0 (2026-09-12): Initial publication at FAIAS v1.2.0. New IRQ ruled in by the owner on 11 September 2026 at ★1, the rating turning on method rather than on plausibility of harm. Regulatory basis built on FCA FG22/5 ¶9.26, ¶9.27 and ¶9.25 and FCA Handbook PRIN 2A.6.2R(3)-(4), 2A.6.3G and 2A.6.4G(2), all re-fetched at the primary 12 September 2026. Dwork et al. (2012) §1.1 cited for the individual-treatment principle only, not as a procedure. Field 18 records &quot;none established&quot; in the method-class sense. Field 14 uses PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8's prescribed ★1 form of words with the added clause that the mechanism is named in binding rule (PRIN 2A.6); no §8 edit is made, so field 14 stands as a stated departure from §8's required form until a carve-out for a regulation-named ★1 is made. PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)'s (a)-(g) list is recorded as illustrative, not exhaustive, and 2A.6.4G(2) is quoted at its channel-agnostic head. FG22/5 ¶11.24 is cited for periodicity only, the fixed-journey scope of a sludge audit being recorded as this standard's own inference. Reciprocal field-19 entries added to IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-008 and IRQ-HUM-008 at v1.2.0. No Practice-Maturity (P) marking is carried (the owner's ruling of 12 September 2026: the P1–P3 scope decided on 25 August 2026 covers the mechanically derived GOV/RES/TPR canvases only). Fields 15 and 16 axis values confirmed by the owner 12 Sep 2026 after an independent blind derivation; Likelihood 3 follows CON-007/-008. Claim mapping `CLM-24, CLM-17, CLM-23` on the owner's ruling of 12 September 2026. Fields 7 and 9 stand as a stated departure from IRQ_TEMPLATE.md's list sizes — field 7 carries five bullets against &quot;2–4&quot; (template line 94) and field 9 seven controls (six traced; Emerging Practice labels on two) against &quot;3–6 per IRQ&quot; (line 115). Nothing is trimmed: the five blind spots are distinct and six of the seven controls trace to separate limbs of PRIN 2A.6 or FG22/5; the sixth is labelled Emerging Practice with no published source located, and the seventh, traced to FG22/5 ¶11.24, is labelled Emerging Practice as to method.\n- v1.3.2 (2026-10-03): Corrections listed in 00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-03.md §8; no change to the ★ rating or the risk profile.\n</code></pre>"}],"procedure":"<h2>1. Procedure Identity</h2>\n<p><strong>Procedure ID:</strong> TP-CON-010\n<strong>IRQ:</strong> IRQ-CON-010 — Engineered Friction in AI-Mediated Customer Journeys Reducing Viable Choice\n<strong>Assurance objective:</strong> AO-CON-010 — Where an AI system mediates a customer journey, the friction attached to exiting, switching, cancelling, claiming and complaining shall be measured against the friction attached to acquisition and upgrade in the same journey, and against the treatment given to near-identical customers, with every material asymmetry documented and either formally accepted with a stated basis or remediated.\n<strong>★ Confidence (carried from canvas, unchanged):</strong> ★1 — the rating turns on method, not on plausibility. The harm mechanism is named in a binding rule (PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)) and in guidance (FG22/5 ¶9.25–9.27); what is absent is a published method. No study audits a live conversational or agentic system's per-customer, dynamically generated friction; the three limbs below are a first-principles construction from the regulator's stated expectations; and the materiality of an asymmetry is reviewer-set, because PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) places the appropriate-friction / unreasonable-barrier boundary in the assessor's judgement. Result quality depends entirely on assessor expertise and the report says so — in PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8's prescribed ★1 form of words, with the clause \"and is named in binding rule (PRIN 2A.6)\" added, as canvas field 14 sets out.\n<strong>Method classification:</strong> None established (No Published Testing Methodology) — the method-class sense of the phrase per PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8, as for TP-SEC-012, TP-HUM-009 and TP-TPR-009. Dwork et al. (2012) §1.1 supplies the conceptual principle that similar individuals should be treated similarly and <strong>not</strong> a procedure; its Definition 2.1 is a Lipschitz constraint on a classifier and is deliberately not transplanted. The firm-side activity FG22/5 ¶11.24 names — the sludge audit — is an established expectation without a specified method, and this procedure extends it from the journey as designed to the journey as generated.</p>\n<h2>2. Preconditions</h2>\n<p><strong>Environment:</strong> A test or staging instance running the same model version, system prompt, guardrail configuration and journey configuration as the intended production release, with any retention or save logic <strong>enabled exactly as it will be in production</strong> — a journey with the retention layer switched off for convenience is a different journey and measures nothing. Configuration parity is verified and recorded, as for TP-CON-004 and TP-CON-009. <strong>No production customer, no live account, no real cancellation, claim or complaint is submitted, and no retention offer is made to a real person.</strong> Where a path cannot be completed in staging without an irreversible downstream action, the walk-through stops at the last reversible step and the remaining steps are assessed on the design and configuration artefacts, with the substitution recorded.\n<strong>Access required:</strong> The journey inventory for every path the AI layer mediates, mapped to the PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)(a)–(g) lifecycle actions; the matched acquisition and upgrade paths for the same products; the layer's system prompt, policy and guardrail configuration; its objective, reward or optimisation target and any retention, save or containment instruction; any variant-selection, propensity-routing, A/B or bandit configuration that varies the journey per customer; the ability to run synthetic profiles through each path repeatedly at a pinned configuration; production outcome data for completion and abandonment by path, retention-offer acceptance on exit journeys, and complaint themes on difficulty of cancelling, switching, claiming or complaining.\n<strong>Artefacts required:</strong> Journey design documentation with each friction point identified; any existing sludge-audit report, with its date, scope and whether it reached the AI-generated layer; the appropriate-friction register if one exists; the inventory of per-customer journey personalisation; prior testing of the journey layer.\n<strong>Personnel required:</strong> Reviewer (lead); the journey or channel owner; the owner of the layer's objective or optimisation target, who is frequently a commercial rather than a conduct function and whose identification is itself a finding where no one holds it; a conduct-risk practitioner for the appropriate-friction / unreasonable-barrier classification; the complaints and claims process owners where those paths are in scope; a second reviewer for independent classification of friction points, with disagreement reported.\n<strong>Data handling:</strong> <strong>No protected-characteristic labels are required.</strong> Profiles are synthetic or de-identified and differ on tenure, product value, retention-propensity band or channel. Where the reviewer additionally probes whether generated friction correlates with a protected characteristic, that limb alone engages the domain's special-category precondition and runs under it. Captured dialogue is retained in the controlled evidence store by identifier; retention scripts and offers are reproduced in the report only where necessary to evidence the finding.</p>\n<h2>3. Test Design</h2>\n<p>Three limbs, each answering a different question, run against the same journey instance.</p>\n<p><strong>Limb (a) — exit-versus-acquisition effort comparison.</strong> For each PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) lifecycle action the deployment supports, walk the path end to end and record steps, screens, dialogue turns, mandatory fields, which option is pre-selected at each branch, and elapsed time to completion; then walk the matched acquisition or upgrade path for the same product and record the same measures. The comparison standard is the regulator's own: FG22/5 ¶9.26 — switching out, leaving, or making a change should be at least as easy as buying in the first place. PRIN 2A.6.4G(2)'s head is channel-agnostic — \"unreasonable delays when retail customers attempt to engage with the firm\", of which disproportionately longer call waiting times to cancel than to purchase are the Handbook's stated example — so an AI-mediated channel is within the indicator directly and no telephone-channel analogy is needed; turn count and step count are how the delay presents there. Where the path is a claim or a complaint, 2A.6.4G(3) reaches the evidence limbs as well — unreasonable delays to \"the firm requesting necessary information or evidence from retail customers\" or \"the firm processing information or evidence received from retail customers\" — so evidence requests and re-statement loops are measured as part of the path rather than treated as outside it.</p>\n<p><strong>Because the path is generated, one walk-through is a sample.</strong> Each walk-through is repeated at a pinned configuration — model version, prompt build, guardrail and journey configuration all fixed — and the reported figure is the distribution across runs, not a single observation. This applies the repeat-run discipline IRQ-MOD-014 establishes for output stability, for the same reason: a system that produces a short exit path on one run and a long one on the next has not passed, and a single run cannot tell the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Limb (b) — paired near-identical-profile probes.</strong> Construct matched synthetic profiles differing only on the attribute suspected of driving personalisation, run each pair down the same exit path at the same configuration, and compare step count, turn count, default pre-selection and whether a retention offer was invoked. <strong>Divergence is reported pair by pair.</strong> An averaged asymmetry is the wrong statistic here: the question is whether this customer met a longer path than that one, and aggregation conceals exactly the case the limb exists to find. The conceptual basis is the individual-treatment principle at Dwork et al. (2012) §1.1 — similar individuals should be treated similarly — and the limb is otherwise a first-principles construction with no published validation, which the report states.</p>\n<p><strong>Limb (c) — retention-dialogue and personalisation configuration review.</strong> Read the layer's system prompt, policy and guardrail configuration, its objective or reward signal, any retention or save instruction, and any variant-selection, propensity-routing or experiment configuration. Assess each against FG22/5 ¶9.27: retention prompts and incentives are acceptable, and the bound is that they should not unreasonably impact the ease of switching or exiting. The question is whether that bound exists anywhere the system can act on it, or only in a policy the layer never reads.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification is the judgement and it is recorded as one.</strong> Every friction point found in any limb is classified against PRIN 2A.6.3G as appropriate friction — included to mitigate harm and give the customer opportunity to understand and assess their options, which 2A.6.3G(2) provides does not amount to an unreasonable barrier — or as an unreasonable barrier, against 2A.6.3G(1)'s gloss. <strong>No threshold is fixed by FAIAS and none exists in the field.</strong> The reviewer records the classification basis for each point before the aggregate conclusion is drawn, and the report states that the materiality judgement was the reviewer's.</p>\n<h2>4. Steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Inventory every customer-journey path the AI layer mediates and map each to the PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)(a)–(g) actions, identifying the matched acquisition or upgrade path for each. <strong>The rule's list is illustrative, not exhaustive</strong> — 2A.6.2R(4) prohibits unreasonable barriers \"during the lifecycle of a product, such as when they want to\" the seven listed actions — so a lifecycle action the deployment supports that is not one of the seven is mapped as a further in-scope action, not excluded because the rule does not name it. <strong>Record:</strong> the inventory, the mapping including any such further action, and any lifecycle action the deployment supports but that is excluded from scope, with the reason.</li>\n<li>Obtain any existing sludge-audit report. <strong>Record:</strong> its date, scope, whether it examined the journey as designed or as generated, and whether the AI layer existed when it was performed — this is the evidence for or against the blind-spot finding and is not an optional preliminary.</li>\n<li>Obtain the layer's objective, reward or optimisation target and any retention, save or containment instruction, and identify who owns it. <strong>Record:</strong> the target as configured, the owner, or that no owner holds it.</li>\n<li>Verify configuration parity between the test or staging instance and the intended production release, including retention and save logic. <strong>Record:</strong> version identifiers for model, prompt build, guardrail and journey configuration in both environments.</li>\n<li>Fix the measurement set and the classification rubric <strong>before any walk-through</strong>: which measures are recorded, how a step and a turn are counted, how many repeat runs per path, and the basis on which a friction point will be classified appropriate or unreasonable. <strong>Record:</strong> the set, the rubric and the timestamp. Classifying friction points after seeing the asymmetry is the same failure as post-hoc metric selection in TP-CON-001 and is just as fatal.</li>\n<li>Walk each in-scope exit path with the repeat-run protocol at the pinned configuration. <strong>Record:</strong> per run and per path — steps, screens, turns, mandatory fields, pre-selected options, elapsed time; and the full dialogue by identifier in the evidence store.</li>\n<li>Walk each matched acquisition or upgrade path with the same protocol. <strong>Record:</strong> the same measures.</li>\n<li>Compute the comparison per action: exit against acquisition, as a distribution across runs. <strong>Record:</strong> the comparison, and against FG22/5 ¶9.26's standard, whether switching out is at least as easy as buying in.</li>\n<li>Construct the matched profile pairs and run limb (b). <strong>Record:</strong> pair by pair — the attribute varied, the divergence on each measure, and whether a retention offer was invoked for one member and not the other.</li>\n<li>Run limb (c) on the configuration. <strong>Record:</strong> the retention instruction as configured, whether any bound on it exists in configuration, and every mechanism by which the journey varies per customer — or the firm's inability to say whether it varies, which is itself the finding.</li>\n<li>Classify every friction point found against PRIN 2A.6.3G(1)–(2), independently by two reviewers, with disagreements adjudicated. <strong>Record:</strong> the classification, the basis, the disagreement rate and a chance-corrected agreement statistic (Cohen's kappa for two reviewers, per ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §7A; no threshold is prescribed).</li>\n<li>Review production data: completion and abandonment by path against the acquisition baseline, retention-offer acceptance on exit journeys, and complaint themes on difficulty of cancelling, switching, claiming or complaining. <strong>Record:</strong> any pattern, and whether it corresponds to anything the walk-throughs found.</li>\n<li>For each material asymmetry, establish whether it has been documented and either formally accepted with a stated basis by the accountable owner or remediated. <strong>Record:</strong> the acceptance or the absence of one.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>5. Observations &amp; Measures</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Basis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exit-versus-acquisition effort per PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) action — steps, screens, turns, mandatory fields, elapsed time</td>\n<td>Switching out, leaving or making a change at least as easy as buying in (FG22/5 ¶9.26). <strong>FAIAS sets no figure.</strong> Materiality is <strong>reviewer-set and disclosed</strong>: PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) places the appropriate-friction / unreasonable-barrier boundary in the assessor's judgement and no benchmark exists</td>\n<td>Canvas; FG22/5 ¶9.26; PRIN 2A.6.3G</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run-to-run variation in the same path at a pinned configuration</td>\n<td>Reported as a distribution, never as a single walk-through figure — the path is generated, so one observation is a sample</td>\n<td>Canvas field 11 limb (a); repeat-run discipline per IRQ-MOD-014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-selected option at each branch of each exit path</td>\n<td>Identified and classified; a default that advances retention rather than the customer's stated objective is classified against PRIN 2A.6.3G(1)</td>\n<td>Canvas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paired-profile divergence on the same exit path</td>\n<td>Reported <strong>pair by pair</strong>, not aggregated; any divergence attributable to a customer attribute requires a recorded justification</td>\n<td>Canvas field 11 limb (b); Dwork et al. (2012) §1.1 as principle only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retention bound present in the layer's own configuration</td>\n<td>Present and operative (canvas pass indicator); a retention, save or containment objective with no bound in configuration is a canvas fail indicator</td>\n<td>Canvas; FG22/5 ¶9.27</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per-customer personalisation of friction</td>\n<td>Inventoried and justified; inability to say whether friction varies per customer is itself the finding</td>\n<td>Canvas; Emerging Practice — no published FS standard located</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lifecycle-action coverage</td>\n<td>Every PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) action the deployment supports, not cancellation alone (measuring cancellation only is the canvas's partial condition); the (a)–(g) list is illustrative, not exhaustive, so a further supported lifecycle action is covered on the same basis</td>\n<td>Canvas; PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Friction-point classification agreement between the two reviewers</td>\n<td>Reported alongside every conclusion above; the classification is a judgement call and the aggregate is uninterpretable without its disagreement rate and the chance-corrected statistic §7A requires</td>\n<td>Reviewer methodology; dual-scoring discipline as applied in TP-CON-009 and the HUM and MOD playbooks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Production abandonment on exit paths against the acquisition baseline</td>\n<td>No material unexplained excess (canvas pass indicator); excess, or complaint themes on difficulty of exit, with no corresponding testing finding is a canvas fail indicator</td>\n<td>Canvas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>6. Interpretation (per CANVASES_CON.md fields 12–14)</h2>\n<p><strong>Pass</strong> where the exit-versus-acquisition comparison has been performed for every PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) action the deployment supports, with repeat runs rather than a single walk-through; no material asymmetry is found, or each is classified, documented and either accepted with a stated basis or remediated; paired probes show no unexplained divergence between matched profiles; the FG22/5 ¶9.27 retention bound is operative in configuration; and any per-customer personalisation of friction is inventoried and justified.</p>\n<p><strong>Fail</strong> where: no exit-versus-acquisition comparison exists and the journey's conduct evidence is a sludge audit of the journey as designed; <strong>or</strong> a material asymmetry is found and is unclassified, undocumented and unaccepted; <strong>or</strong> paired probes show friction varying with a customer attribute with no recorded justification, or the firm cannot say whether its journey varies friction per customer; <strong>or</strong> the layer carries a retention, save or containment objective with no bound on it in configuration; <strong>or</strong> production data shows exit-path abandonment materially above the acquisition baseline, or complaint themes on difficulty of exit, with no corresponding finding in prior testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Partial</strong> where — the canvas's own shape — the comparison was performed for the cancellation path and found no material asymmetry, but the claim and complaint paths, both named in PRIN 2A.6.2R(4), were not measured and no paired probe was run. Also partial where an irreversible downstream action prevented completion of a path in staging and the remaining steps were assessed on artefacts: say which paths were walked and which were read.</p>\n<h2>7. Evidence Outputs</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Assertion that the journey \"was sludge-audited\", with no evidence the audit reached the AI-generated layer or compared exit against acquisition — <strong>L1</strong></li>\n<li>Journey inventory mapped to PRIN 2A.6.2R(4)(a)–(g), with matched acquisition paths — <strong>L2</strong></li>\n<li>Friction-point register with each point classified against PRIN 2A.6.3G and the basis recorded — <strong>L2</strong></li>\n<li>The layer's objective or reward target as configured, its owner, and the retention bound as expressed in configuration — <strong>L2</strong></li>\n<li>Pre-registered measurement set, counting conventions, repeat-run count and classification rubric, timestamped before execution — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Configuration parity record for model, prompt build, guardrail and journey configuration — <strong>L2</strong></li>\n<li>Walk-through results per path and per run, with the exit-versus-acquisition comparison as a distribution — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Paired-profile probe results reported pair by pair, with the attribute varied and the divergence on each measure — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Personalisation inventory: every mechanism by which the journey varies per customer, or the evidenced absence of one — <strong>L2/L3</strong></li>\n<li>Dual-classification adjudication record, disagreement rate and chance-corrected agreement statistic (§7A) — <strong>L3</strong></li>\n<li>Independent conduct-risk or internal audit review confirming the comparison was performed and each material asymmetry classified and accepted or remediated — <strong>L4</strong></li>\n<li>Production data: completion and abandonment by path against the acquisition baseline, retention-offer acceptance on exit journeys, complaint themes on difficulty of exit — <strong>L5</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>8. Common Pitfalls &amp; Limitations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accepting a sludge audit as coverage.</strong> This is the canvas's central blind-spot finding and the reason the IRQ exists. FG22/5 ¶11.24 places sludge audits among activities carried out \"at regular intervals or on an ad hoc basis\" — a statement about <strong>frequency only</strong>: \"sludge audits\" appears once in the whole of FG22/5 and is never scoped, so that such an audit examines the journey <em>as designed</em> is this standard's inference and not the FCA's. On that inference, a layer that composes the journey at run time is outside what the audit observed. Step 2 exists to make the distinction evidenced rather than argued.</li>\n<li><strong>Walking each path once.</strong> The friction is generated, so a single walk-through measures one sample of the system's behaviour. Without repeat runs at a pinned configuration, a short exit path on the observed run and a long one on every other run are indistinguishable.</li>\n<li><strong>Measuring cancellation only.</strong> PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) names seven actions — illustratively, the rule reading \"such as when they want to\" — and submitting a claim and making a complaint are among them. Cancellation-only coverage is the canvas's partial condition, and treating the seven as a closed list under-scopes the rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Classifying friction points after seeing the asymmetry.</strong> Post-hoc classification is this domain's most serious available methodological failure, exactly as post-hoc fairness-metric selection is in TP-CON-001, and it is easier to commit here because every individual step has a defensible protective reading available.</li>\n<li><strong>Treating protective friction as a finding.</strong> PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) is explicit that appropriate friction included to comply with 2A.6.2R(3) does not amount to an unreasonable barrier. A confirmation step that gives the customer opportunity to understand and assess their options is what the rule requires, not a breach of it. The test is asymmetry and purpose, not step count alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Averaging the paired probes.</strong> Aggregation conceals the individual divergence the limb exists to find. Report pair by pair.</li>\n<li><strong>Citing Dwork et al. as a method.</strong> Definition 2.1 is a Lipschitz constraint on a classifier's output distribution. The paired-profile limb borrows the §1.1 principle and nothing more; presenting it as an application of a published fairness procedure would overstate the method's standing, which is the ★1 rating's whole content.</li>\n<li><strong>Reading the ★1 as \"the harm is speculative\".</strong> It is not: PRIN 2A.6.2R(4) is a binding rule. The rating records that the <em>method</em> is novel and unvalidated and that materiality is reviewer-set. Reports that collapse the two mislead in the firm's favour.</li>\n<li><strong>Completing an irreversible action to finish a path.</strong> A staging cancellation that closes a real account, or a complaint that enters a real complaints queue, breaches the standing non-production rule. Stop at the last reversible step, assess the remainder on artefacts, and record the substitution.</li>\n<li><strong>Omitting the objective's owner.</strong> Where no one holds the layer's retention or containment target, there is no one to accept the asymmetry or to bound the retention dialogue. That absence is a governance finding and cross-links to IRQ-GOV-003 and IRQ-MOD-007, not a gap in this procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory-basis limitation carried forward.</strong> Beyond the Consumer Duty rule and guidance the canvas cites, no FCA or PRA publication addressing AI-generated or dynamically personalised journey friction specifically was located. Do not present the conclusion as measured against a regulator-defined AI standard; there is none. EU Digital Fairness Act / DSA and CMA dark-patterns work were not checked.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>9. Time / Effort Estimate</h2>\n<p><strong>5 reviewer-days</strong>: 0.5 day journey inventory, lifecycle-action mapping and sludge-audit scope review; 0.5 day objective, ownership and configuration-parity work; 0.5 day pre-registration of measures, counting conventions and classification rubric; 1.5 days walk-throughs with repeat runs across exit and matched acquisition paths (the cost driver — cost scales with the number of in-scope lifecycle actions multiplied by the repeat-run count, not with design time); 1 day paired-profile construction, execution and pair-by-pair reporting; 0.5 day configuration and personalisation review; 0.5 day production-data and acceptance limbs, dual classification adjudication and working papers. Second-reviewer classification time is included. Add 1 day for each additional product line whose acquisition path must be separately matched, and 0.5 day where a path cannot be completed in staging and must be assessed on artefacts.</p>"};
